{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "03B4", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$G$", "MATH_0002": "$X$", "MATH_0003": "$H^1(X, G)$", "MATH_0004": "$G$", "MATH_0005": "$H^2(X, G)$", "MATH_0006": "$G$", "MATH_0007": "$G$", "MATH_0008": "$H^2(X, G)$", "MATH_0009": "$G$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:05936dfde81b9b5bc33819dcd4fbee37ed58b3142d671d0d4fce50652da6f010", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:e9a6a4cd807c3132f50534c26c13b94ccfd13a5a9e3a0dc4b3f46d8f37e86fc0", "source_text": "The book discusses fibered categories, stacks, torsors and gerbes over general sites but does not discuss algebraic stacks. For instance, if <MATH_0001> is a sheaf of abelian groups on <MATH_0002>, then in the same way <MATH_0003> can be identified with <MATH_0004>-torsors, <MATH_0005> can be identified with an appropriately defined set of <MATH_0006>-gerbes. When <MATH_0007> is not abelian, then <MATH_0008> is defined as the set of <MATH_0009>-gerbes.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:76a18cf396c858ee5bf259cca3581d6020f341d20689553e602a7848f8f40d1f", "unit_id": "tag:03B4:item004-quote"}
